---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Ceramics booking for schools and colleges"
	description="Booking software for school and college ceramics departments: wheel time between classes, kiln firing queues for one or two kilns, and courses that follow your semester."
>
	<section class="band-dark page-hero">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">For schools &amp; colleges</p>
			<h1>A ceramics room that runs on the timetable, not the whiteboard.</h1>
			<p class="lede narrow">
				One or two kilns, a semester of courses, and students who want a wheel in the hour between
				lectures. Kilnbook keeps wheel time, firings and classes in one place, so the technician is
				not the booking system.
			</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Wheels booked between classes</h2>
		<p>
			Students reserve wheels and hand-building tables in one-hour blocks from their phone. You
			decide how many hours each group gets a week and how far ahead they can book, so open studio
			time is shared fairly instead of going to whoever gets there first. A tablet by the door shows
			who is in the room. <a href="/features/wheel-time">How wheel time works</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>One queue per kiln</h2>
		<p>
			Students log a piece when they put it on the bisque shelf, and Kilnbook adds it to the right
			load for each kiln. You see how full every bisque and glaze load is, and each kiln keeps a
			firing log with the date, cone, schedule and who loaded it. When a load comes out, students
			get a text telling them their work is ready. <a href="/features/firings">How firing queues
			work</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Courses that follow the semester</h2>
		<p>
			Set up each course as a fixed series, like Wheel 101 on Tuesdays, and add drop-in sessions
			for open studio. Instructors get their own login with the day's roster and can mark
			attendance from the studio tablet. <a href="/features/classes">Classes and packs</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Which plan fits a department?</h2>
		<p>
			Most departments with one or two kilns fit on the Studio plan, which covers up to 3 kilns with
			unlimited members, wheel and table reservations, and pickup texts. School ceramics programs
			get 30% off any plan. <a href="/pricing">Compare plans</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Questions departments ask</h2>
		<div class="faq">
			<details>
				<summary>We only have one kiln. Is Kilnbook still worth it?</summary>
				<p>
					Yes. The Starter plan covers one kiln, and the Studio plan covers up to 3 kilns if you also
					want wheel and table reservations. A single kiln shared by a whole department is often
					where a firing queue saves the most time.
				</p>
			</details>
			<details>
				<summary>Do you offer education pricing?</summary>
				<p>
					School ceramics programs and registered nonprofits get 30% off any plan. Mention it when
					you book a demo.
				</p>
			</details>
			<details>
				<summary>Can students book wheels outside scheduled classes?</summary>
				<p>
					Yes. Wheel time is booked separately from classes, so students can reserve an hour of open
					studio between lectures within the limits you set.
				</p>
			</details>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="cta-band">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Get the ceramics room off the sign-up sheet.</h2>
			<p>See how Kilnbook handles your kilns, wheels and semester courses.</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-dark">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
